Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.7.59", "inferred_role": "user_workstation_target_of_privilege_grant", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:18:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-08T01:28:18+0100", "agent_name": "WS-023"},
    {"ip": "10.30.5.18", "inferred_role": "it_support_source_workstation", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:18:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-08T01:18:42+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "At 2028-01-08 01:18:42 CET, Event 4624 'An account was successfully logged on' fired on WS-023 (10.30.7.59): logonType=10 (RemoteInteractive = RDP), TargetUserName=tech.support, IpAddress=10.30.5.18 (IT-Support workstation in 10.30.5.0/24 helpdesk subnet), AuthenticationPackageName=Negotiate. The session looks legitimate at the protocol layer — it is a domain IT-Support account connecting from an IT-Support workstation. There is no failed-logon precursor (no 4625 burst), no off-subnet origin, no abnormal hour pattern for IT-Support shifts. The credential is either being used by its legitimate owner OR by an attacker who already controls the tech.support account.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 (rule.id 60224). The session itself is not suspicious in isolation — the suspicion comes ENTIRELY from what this session does next.", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:18:42+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation", "technique": "T1098 + T1136.001", "what_happened": "At 01:21:54 CET (~3 minutes 12 seconds into the tech.support session), Sysmon Event 1 captured: image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\net.exe, commandLine='net.exe localgroup administrators john.doe /add', parentImage=C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe, user=CORP\\tech.support, on WS-023. A standard end-user account (john.doe) is being added to the LOCAL Administrators group of a workstation — granting administrative privileges on that endpoint without requiring an exploit. This is the textbook Account Manipulation technique: the attacker (or insider) leverages an already-privileged session to mint NEW privilege for a SECOND account, establishing an elevated foothold that survives any cleanup of the original entry point.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=net.exe + commandLine contains 'localgroup administrators' + '/add' + parentImage=cmd.exe (interactive, not scripted/scheduled) + user=CORP\\tech.support. Diagnostic of T1098 Account Manipulation.", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:21:54+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation", "technique": "T1098", "what_happened": "At 01:22:19 CET (25 seconds after the net.exe command), Windows Security Event 4732 'A member was added to a security-enabled local group' fired on WS-023: TargetUserName=john.doe, GroupName=Administrators, SubjectUserName=tech.support, MemberSID=S-1-5-21-...-john.doe-SID, TargetSid (GroupSID)=S-1-5-32-544 (BUILTIN\\Administrators well-known SID — the actual LOCAL ADMIN group, not a renamed lookalike). This is the kernel-level confirmation that the privilege grant was COMMITTED — it did not fail, it did not get blocked by a GPO, the user IS now in BUILTIN\\Administrators on this host.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4732 (rule.id 60132, level 8). GroupSID=S-1-5-32-544 = BUILTIN\\Administrators (immutable well-known SID). The pairing (Sysmon Event 1 net.exe + Security Event 4732 with matching MemberSID 25 seconds later) is the canonical signature of a local-admin add operation. Linkage: agent.name + subjectUserName + targetUserName + time window.", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:22:19+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Out-of-Process Approval", "technique": "T1098", "what_happened": "At 01:22:22 CET (3 seconds after the 4732), the Wazuh AD integration ran a CHANGE_TICKET_LOOKUP enrichment against the ITSM ticketing system, querying for any approved change-request, JIT/PAM grant, or help-desk ticket authorizing a local-admin addition for john.doe on WS-023 within ±24h of the event. The lookup returned NO MATCHING TICKET. The corporate policy requires that any local-admin grant be tied to a documented ticket (either ITSM change-request or PAM-orchestrated approval). The absence of a ticket means this addition is OUT-OF-BAND — it bypassed the change-control workflow that exists specifically to prevent unauthorized privilege grants.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule (rule.id 100732) — chains the Event 4732 with an HTTP enrichment to ITSM (ServiceNow / Jira ITSM equivalent). Returns 'ticket_match: false' → the alert is escalated. The architectural detection is exactly this: 'a privilege grant happened AND no ticket exists to justify it'.", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:22:22+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Test of Granted Privilege", "technique": "T1078.003", "what_happened": "At 01:28:14 CET (~5 minutes 55 seconds after the 4732), Event 4624 fired on WS-023 with logonType=2 (Interactive — physical console or KVM, not RDP), TargetUserName=john.doe. The logonType=2 indicates the login happened AT the keyboard of WS-023 (or via a remote console viewer that maps as type 2). This pattern — privilege grant followed shortly by an interactive console login by the newly-privileged account — is highly diagnostic: the attacker (or insider) is testing that the grant worked. They are NOT just running the net command and walking away; they are validating that john.doe really did inherit local-admin power.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 logonType=2, targetUserName=john.doe, on WS-023, occurring AFTER the 4732 grant. The temporal proximity (5m55s) and the logonType=2 (console, not network) are the signature.", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:28:14+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Confirmation of Elevation", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "At 01:28:18 CET (4 seconds after the john.doe console login), Event 4672 'Special privileges assigned to new logon' fired on WS-023: SubjectUserName=john.doe, PrivilegeList includes SeBackupPrivilege, SeRestorePrivilege, SeDebugPrivilege. Event 4672 is the canonical 'this token is now admin-tier' signal — Windows logs it ONLY when a logon session is granted ANY of the sensitive privileges (Backup/Restore/Debug/TakeOwnership/etc). SeDebugPrivilege in particular is the privilege required to attach a debugger to LSASS for credential dumping. The kernel itself is confirming: john.doe's new session has admin-tier privileges.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4672 (rule.id 60106). SubjectUserName=john.doe + presence of SeDebugPrivilege in PrivilegeList. Closes the chain: the privilege grant (4732) is operationally validated by Windows itself (4672).", "first_seen": "2028-01-08T01:28:18+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-01-08T01:18:42+0100", "end": "2028-01-08T01:28:18+0100"},
  "alert_count": 6,
  "unique_steps": 6
}
